WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.980
Safe process invocation begins with selecting the intended executable.

2
00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:14.840
If a script relies casually on command lookup, aliases, functions, wrapper scripts, or an uncontrolled PATH, it may run something different than the author expected.

3
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:24.780
That risk is especially important in automation that runs under service accounts, CI runners, containers, or scheduled task environments.

4
00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.900
PATH is convenient, but it is also part of the trust boundary.

5
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:33.240
It determines where Bash searches when a command name is used.

6
00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.420
For low-risk local helpers, default lookup may be acceptable.

7
00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:48.080
For high-impact automation, teams should understand how required tools are found, which locations are trusted, and what happens if a tool is missing or shadowed by another executable.

8
00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.320
Wrappers and helper scripts need review too.

9
00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:57.500
A wrapper can be useful when it standardizes options or environment, but it can also hide behavior.

10
00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:08.320
If a script invokes a helper, reviewers should know what that helper does, how it is versioned, and whether it changes arguments, output, logging, or exit status semantics.

11
00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:12.680
Avoid letting runtime input provide arbitrary command names.

12
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:18.860
If the script supports multiple tools or actions, map a validated mode to known code paths.

13
00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:24.800
That lets the input choose among supported behavior without controlling executable selection directly.

14
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.740
It also makes tests and permissions easier to reason about.

15
00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:33.820
The goal is not to hard-code every absolute path in every script.

16
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.000
The goal is deliberate selection.

17
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:46.820
A reviewer should be able to tell what program will run, why that program is trusted, what version assumptions matter, and how the script behaves if the expected executable is not available.
